PRE-TABLE CHECK -- gdromexitsix Round 2
=======================================
Written 2026-08-28, BEFORE table 1 of this round has run.  This file's mtime is the
order proof the mandate asks for; if a kill-table log in this directory predates it,
the mandate was not met.

THIS IS THE FIRST APPLICATION OF THE MANDATE (carrier, adopted 7084b36).  Under the
freshly-minted-exemplar rule it therefore owes one extra thing, discharged at the
bottom: it must CHECK the CHANNEL line's own write-time-knowability claim rather than
simply obey it.

THE DIFF THIS COVERS (not yet written):
  Site A -- the case 0x30 data-format check.  Anchor: `unimplemented data format 0x%02x`
            (the SECOND occurrence, the one followed by exit(1); the first is the 0x24
            branch, which already survives).
  Site B -- the default: arm.  Anchor: `handle_command: unimplemented command`
  Both de-fatalised: keep the diagnostic behind a per-site latch bit, drop exit(1),
  `break` to the function's common tail.  Site A's break goes INSIDE the else-if and
  BEFORE `sector_nr =`, leaving the 0x24 branch byte-identical.


1. SIBLING PARITY
-----------------
Site B's nearest sibling is `case 0x70` (MOUNT, per NetBSD's gdrom_mount_disk) -- the only
implemented command that allocates no data, and a command a real driver ISSUES rather than a
stub nobody calls.  VERIFIED by reading: the switch carries exactly three case labels
(0x14, 0x30, 0x70) plus default:, and 0x70's body is a comment and a break.  Both it and a de-fatalised `default:` break to the same common tail:

    if (d->data != NULL)  d->cond |= COND_DATA_AVAIL;
    else                { d->cnt = 0;  d->cond &= ~COND_DATA_AVAIL; }

*** SO THE PARITY CHECK FINDS A SYMMETRY, NOT AN ASYMMETRY, AND THAT IS THE WORSE
FINDING.  After the edit, a DECLINED UNKNOWN COMMAND and a LEGITIMATE IMPLEMENTED
NO-DATA COMMAND are observationally identical on the tail channel. ***  Any row whose
oracle is "cnt == 0 and COND_DATA_AVAIL clear" therefore cannot attribute what it saw.
The only thing that separates them is the LATCH -- the once-only diagnostic fires for
the decline and never for 0x70.

MEASURED GAP: `0x70` appears ZERO times in gdrom_default_probe.py (grep -c '0x70' = 0).
So there is no sibling baseline row to compare against.

ROW OWED (S1): a `0x70` row establishing that the tail channel reads identically for a
legitimate no-data command.  Without it, the declined-default row's tail reading is
un-attributable -- it proves the guest reached SOME no-data path, not that it was
declined.


2. CLAIM -> ROW
---------------
DEFERRED TO WRITE TIME, and recorded as owed rather than passed -- the diff's comments
do not exist yet, and a check run against nothing is the check-that-cannot-fail shape.

The trigger in force is the SEMANTIC one adopted in 7084b36, not the old verb list:
every added sentence asserting an OBSERVABLE CONSEQUENCE owes a row or an explicit
no-row sentence, counterfactual "would <consequence>" clauses included.  That repair
exists precisely because #489's load-bearing comment contained none of the five verbs.

TO RUN: after the comments are written, before table 1.


3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
----------------------------
Site A.  Guard reads d->cmd[1].  Guarded body reads d->cmd[2..4] and d->cmd[8..10].
The fields DIFFER, so the check asks whether they are written at the same sites.

    MEASURED: they are.  d->cmd[12] is filled two bytes per store
    (`d->cmd[d->cmd_count++] = idata;` / `= idata >> 8;`) and handle_command dispatches
    only at cmd_count == 12, so all twelve bytes arrive from the same six word-writes
    before any dispatch.  No path writes a partial packet: the >= 12 arm exits, and
    narrow access is DELIBERATELY not emulated -- with a measured reason recorded in
    the file (letting cmd_count step by 1 makes 11 byte writes + 1 word write go
    11 -> 13, skipping the == 12 equality and reaching an otherwise-dead exit).

  => NO DISAGREEING STATE EXISTS for the cmd[] fields.  N/A, reason recorded.  NO ROW OWED.

*** BUT THE ENUMERATION STOPPED EARLY, and the fable5 seat caught it.  AMENDED 2026-08-28
BEFORE TABLE 1. ***  Site A's guarded body reads more than cmd[2..4] and cmd[8..10]: the
protected continuation also reads **`d->cnt`**, which is written at the CNTLO/CNTHI
register sites -- A DIFFERENT SITE from the command packet.  By this check's own letter
("where they differ, grep whether they are written at the same sites"), the cmd[1]-vs-cnt
question was owed and was not asked.

THE ANSWER IS BENIGN AND NO NEW ROW IS OWED -- the disagreeing states are already carried
by existing cnt rows (`nodata len`, `stale says`, the OB-20 mismatch decline).  **But the
omission propagates into the kill table**: clause (d)'s bound draws operand swaps ONLY from
fields the guarded BODY reads, so the mutant enumeration MUST include cnt-conditioned forms
such as `d->cmd[1] != 0x20 && d->cnt != 0`.  Recorded here so table 1 carries them.

Site B.  Guard reads d->cmd[0]; the fatal() argument reads d->cmd[0]; the switch
dispatches on d->cmd[0].  Guard field == body field.

  => N/A, no row owed.

(Note this is the check that paid off at #485, where the default: arm's fatal()
arguments WERE the guarded body's field reads and enumerated D1..D5 mechanically.  Here
the same reading discharges rather than generates -- which is the check working, not
the check idling, and the distinction is why N/A must carry its reason.)


4. CHANNEL  -- the mutant each row kills, and the observable that differs
------------------------------------------------------------------------
*** THIS IS WHERE THE ROUND'S REAL OBLIGATION IS, AND IT IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. ***

FROZEN CHALLENGE SET, run against the COMMITTED build before any edit
(C0_correct.raw, C1_badcmd0.raw in this directory):

    C0  correct packet 0x2030                    58/58     ctlOK=1   baseline
    C1  cmd[0]=0x31 -> default: arm              55/58     ctlOK=1   CAUGHT
    C2  cmd[1]=0x21 -> format check              55/58     ctlOK=1   CAUGHT

FREEZE COMPLETE.  BOTH mutants are caught on the committed build, and caught by the SAME
three rows -- so the two sites share one oracle and one blinding.

The three rows that fall under C1, by diff of C0 against C1:

    nodata stale     survived -> died
    stale says       1        -> died
    stale idle       0x0      -> died

ALL THREE DETECT THE MUTANT BY THE HOST DYING.  Their named channel is process
termination.

    => AFTER ROUND 2 DE-FATALISES default:, THE HOST DOES NOT DIE.  All three revert to
       their C0 values and the probe scores 58/58 ON A MIS-ASSEMBLED PACKET.

That is the CHANNEL check's rejection criterion met exactly: a row whose named channel
cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous BY CONSTRUCTION.  Here it is not merely
vacuous after the edit -- it is ACTIVELY MISLEADING, because 58/58 is also what a
correct packet scores, so a future maintainer whose test packet rots gets a clean run.

AND THE PROBE'S OWN HEADER ALREADY SAID SO, at :90-96, before this round existed:

    "*** THE SURVIVAL ROWS DETECT PROCESS TERMINATION ONLY. ***  `arm survive`,
     `nodata live` and `nodata stale` compute "survived" if alive else "died", and
     `alive` is False ONLY when a read raises OSError or returns empty INSIDE a budget
     window -- so they are true of an emulator that is HUNG, one that is FROZEN, and,
     measured, one that has ALREADY BEEN KILLED."

This is the #489 pattern repeating: the governing sentence was in the file before the
defect was found, and nothing consulted it at row-writing time.

*** L4 CORRECTION, 2026-08-28, SAME DAY: THE HEADER QUOTE ABOVE IS THE WRONG WARRANT FOR
THIS CLAIM, AND A PANEL SEAT (glm-5.3-flash) CAUGHT IT.  The claim SURVIVES on better
evidence; the citation did not. ***

The header names the survival rows as `arm survive`, `nodata live` and `nodata stale`.
The rows that actually FELL under C1/C2 are `nodata stale`, `stale says` and `stale idle`.
**ONLY ONE NAME OVERLAPS.**  So the header sentence does not establish that all three
falling rows are alive-gated, which is what it was cited for -- either its triple is not
exhaustive, or the probe's self-description is stale.

THE CORRECT WARRANT IS THE THREE ROW DEFINITIONS THEMSELVES, each carrying the SAME
`if alive else "died"` guard, read directly:

    row("nodata stale", "survived" if alive else "died", "survived", ...)
    row("stale says",   ("%d" % len(DMA_NOD.findall(t))) if alive else "died", "1", ...)
    row("stale idle",   printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died", "0x0", ...)

That is STRONGER than the header sentence: it is the mechanism rather than a description
of it, and it is per-row rather than a list that can go stale.

**AND THE SECOND HALF OF THE SEAT'S CHALLENGE WAS SETTLED BY READING THE SESSION.**  It
asked whether the C1 session PRIMES with a data-allocating command first -- because if it
does not, `stale says` would read 0 post-edit, the row would STILL fall, and the blindness
claim would be false.  The session stores `r8=0x2030` (cmd[0]=0x30, cmd[1]=0x20) then FIVE
zero words, so `sector_count == 0` and the command takes the sector_count-reject path:
**`d->data` is NULL either way.**  A declined `default:` therefore reaches the same tail
with the same NULL, `stale says` still counts one DMA_NOD match, and `stale idle` still
reads 0x0.  The blindness claim HOLDS, now for a reason that was measured rather than
assumed.

*Recorded here rather than silently fixed: this artifact was committed in c2249eb, and the
late-dissent rule says closure changes the LICENSE, never the truth-value -- a wrong record
is L4 and gets corrected in place.*

ROWS OWED (C1..C3), all before the product edit:
  C1  a DEATH-MODE channel.  gdrom_default_probe.py's session() calls waitpid and
      DISCARDS the status (filed as gdromdefaultstatusless), so no row can distinguish
      a clean exit from a signal from a decline.  Capture and expose it.
  C2  a LATCH row: the declined default: fires its once-only diagnostic exactly once,
      and 0x70 never fires it.  This is the only channel that survives the edit and
      still separates decline from legitimate-no-data (see check 1).
  C3  the S1 sibling row from check 1.

STRENGTHENING, NOT ORACLE-CORRECTION -- and the discriminator was applied, not assumed.
The mechanical trigger is "does any shipped row flip colour under the CORRECT new
code?"  Here YES: the nodata stale triple flips green.  But they flip green because the
code stopped doing the thing they observed, not because they pinned the wrong
expectation -- so the challenge set is FROZEN FIRST and shown caught on the committed
build (above), then the edit lands, then the same mutants must still be caught on the
NAMED replacement channel.  Re-pinning after the edit would be indistinguishable from
fitting the test to the binary.


CHECKING THE CHANNEL LINE ITSELF (owed by the freshly-minted-exemplar rule)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CHANNEL's claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable AT WRITING TIME,
before any table runs.

*** THIS SELF-GRADE WAS OVERCLAIMED AND THE MTIMES REFUTE IT.  Corrected 2026-08-28 the
same day, by the fable5 seat, using this round's OWN artifacts as the evidence. ***

WHAT I ORIGINALLY WROTE, kept verbatim because a correction must quote what it refutes:

  | VERDICT: THE CLAIM HELD HERE ... The measurement (C0 vs C1) CONFIRMED what the
  | reading predicted; it did not produce it.

THE ORDER EVIDENCE RUNS THE OTHER WAY:

    C0_correct.raw / C1_badcmd0.raw   15:19:51
    PRETABLE.out finalised            15:36:09      -- SIXTEEN MINUTES LATER

**No artifact anywhere records a pre-15:19:51 prediction of WHICH ROWS FALL**, and check 4's
own wording concedes it: *"The three rows that fall under C1, BY DIFF OF C0 AGAINST C1"* --
that is a measurement PRODUCING the row list, not confirming it.  This is the
order-discriminator class -- the same rule this project applies to score claims and to
oracle re-pins -- turned on my own self-grade, and I failed it.

*** THE HONEST GRADE, WHICH SPLITS THE CLAIM IN TWO: ***

  * THE CHANNEL IDENTITY -- that these rows' oracle is PROCESS TERMINATION -- was knowable
    at write time, and was in fact ALREADY RECORDED IN GIT FOUR DAYS EARLIER: the probe
    header sentence entered the tree in `7c9dd9e` (2026-08-24, #462), by a different
    round's author.  VERIFIED here with `git log -S`, not asserted.  **That is STRONGER
    evidence than my narrative was**, precisely because it does not depend on me.
  * THE ROW-LEVEL ENUMERATION -- which three rows fall -- was MEASURED, not predicted.

So CHANNEL's write-time-knowability survives for the CHANNEL and not for the ROW LIST, and
the distinction matters: the check claims a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at
writing time, which is about the channel.  A future round applying CHANNEL should record
its prediction BEFORE running anything, which is the only way this grade could have been
earned rather than argued.

*Author-grades-own-application is exactly the hazard here, and it is why the freshly-minted-
exemplar rule asks the first application to CHECK the rule.  I checked it and graded myself
generously; a seat with the mtimes settled it in one pass.*

No kill table was run, and none was needed for check 4.

Two honest limits, because one application cannot settle a rule:
  * This site is unusually favourable -- the rows' channel is literally named in the
    probe's own header, so "what observable does this row read?" required no inference.
    A row whose oracle is a computed value would be harder.
  * The check told me the rows are vacuous AFTER the edit.  It did not, by itself, tell
    me what to replace them with; that came from check 1's sibling reading.  So CHANNEL
    is a rejection filter, exactly as its bounded form claims, and NOT a generator --
    which is the property that keeps it from becoming #392.


