PASS 1 ON THE FULL ROUND (panel_20260828_182817) -- THE ROW SET IS SITE-ASYMMETRIC
================================================================================

7 answered, 1 walled (kimi CLI, covered by kimi-k3:cloud), 0 down.  Fable 5 is
QUOTA-WALLED on two firings and is recorded as a seat failure by name.

*** FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY FOUND ONE STRUCTURAL DEFECT, AND IT IS MINE: ***

    kimi-k3:  "the detector is site-asymmetric: every decline-OBSERVING row is
               written against Site B."

The fix treats both sites identically.  The ROW SET gave Site B three
decline-observing rows -- `unk cnt`, `unk event`, `unk x2` -- and Site A NONE of the
equivalents.  deepseek and glm each built the same escape from that gap without
having seen each other's answer; minimax noted separately that `unk event` is
Site-B-only and the brief never says so.

THE ESCAPE THEY ALL BUILT: set Site A's latch bit ONLY in the `0x24` branch, not in
the `else if`.  Repeated bad formats then announce EVERY TIME -- the bounded-announce
guarantee is gone -- and nothing reddens, because `fmt cont` sends ONE bad format and
`both sites` sends one of each.  It is M2nolatch's exact shape at the other site.

kimi listed four more Site-A twins of mutants my table measured only at Site B:
`return` in the else-if; `break` nested inside Site A's latch `if` (so the SECOND
identical format falls through into `sector_nr`); `0x24` symmetrised into a decline
(byte-identical message, invisible to any text matcher); and a Site-A latch aliasing
#489's `0x14` warn bit (my M3 measured A-B sharing only).

ROWS ADDED, named consistently by three seats, all <= 12 chars:
    fmt x2      same bad format twice in one session -> fmt == 1
    fmt cnt     Site-A tail pin: CNT == 0 after the decline (kills the Site-A `return`)
    fmt event   SYSASIC_EVENT_GDROM set on a declined bad format
Denominator moves 58 -> ~71, so BOTH the literal `58` and the 58-name `for v in ...`
list in gate_sh_rounding.sh must change together.

*** AND THIS IS A PRE-TABLE CHECK I RAN AND AIMED AT THE WRONG PAIR. ***  SIBLING
PARITY says: for each arm the diff de-fatalises, compare its observable channels
against its nearest sibling.  I compared `default:` against `case 0x70` -- a
NEIGHBOUR -- and never compared SITE A AGAINST SITE B, the two arms the diff itself
de-fatalises.  Those are the siblings the check is about.

    THE CHECK'S FIRST QUESTION IS "WHICH ARMS DOES THIS DIFF DE-FATALISE?" AND THEN
    "DO THEY OBSERVE THE SAME CHANNELS?"  A diff that de-fatalises TWO arms owes that
    comparison between THEM before any neighbour is considered.

Caught at PASS 1, before implementation -- which is what pass 1 is for, and why the
rows and the fix ship together rather than the rows arriving after.

*One factual slip in a dissent, recorded so it is not propagated: deepseek wrote that
`0x2030` is "command 0x20 (not 0x30)".  The data port fills the packet LOW BYTE FIRST,
so `0x2030` gives cmd[0]=0x30, cmd[1]=0x20 -- it IS command 0x30.  Its CONCLUSION (no
probe session completes a 0x30) is right, for the different reason the brief states:
sector_count is 0, so OB-20 rejects before alloc_data.*

